{"kind":"problem","subkind":"problem","paper":"2307.16868","version":"1","local":"kn-1","file":"","section":"sec-1","order":1,"title":"The Knight's Tour Problem","body_md":"Can a knight make a closed tour on an $8 \\times 8$ chessboard visiting each cell exactly once?","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"KN","item":"problem","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"paper.tex","from":49,"to":51},"hash":"sha256:b747b84feb5100f069b302dcb76b53614efdc8fd950211e38a24d85e21b10cb8","minhash":"","census":"2307.16868v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"problem","subkind":"problem","paper":"2307.16868","version":"1","local":"pn-1","file":"","section":"sec-1","order":2,"title":"The Prince's Tour Problem","body_md":"Let $k$ be a positive integer. Can a $k$-prince make a closed tour on an $8 \\times 8$ chessboard visiting each cell exactly once?","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"PN","item":"problem","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"paper.tex","from":65,"to":67},"hash":"sha256:1509c5eb8da5ffaa5d43f9f8338bb31767691f9aa59c1be9086a2764e587f094","minhash":"","census":"2307.16868v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2307.16868","version":"1","local":"prop-1","file":"","label":"prop:all8","number":"1.1","section":"sec-1","order":3,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{prop:all8}\r\nThere exists a $k$-prince's tour on an $8 \\times 8$ chessboard if and only if $k$ is odd and not greater than $7$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"counter","labels":["prop:all8"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"paper.tex","from":82,"to":84},"hash":"sha256:6f0a23cabd32fecc750cc2033b51f897f2b432be6a2ed603d5b7f09f82a480cf","minhash":"","census":"2307.16868v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2307.16868","version":"1","local":"prop-2","file":"","label":"prop:all2468","number":"1.2","section":"sec-1","order":4,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{prop:all2468}\r\nFor $n \\in [1,4]$ there exists  a closed $k$P's tour over a $2n \\times 2n$ chessboard for every odd $k \\leq 2n-1$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"counter","labels":["prop:all2468"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"paper.tex","from":87,"to":89},"hash":"sha256:0939eaff66a559174997f957bc86e819235814fd35c2663ab6200e428360cb1e","minhash":"","census":"2307.16868v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"proof","paper":"2307.16868","version":"1","local":"proof-1","file":"","section":"sec-1","order":5,"title":"Proof","body_md":"As already remarked, there exists a closed $1$P's tour on a  $2n \\times 2n$ chessboard for every integer $n\\geq 1$. If $n = 2$, then we need to check the existence of a closed $3P$'s  tour over a $4 \\times 4$ chessboard (we remark that a closed knight's tour on this chessboard does not exist, see \\cite{W}); an example of such a tour is the following:\r\n\\[\r\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|} \\hline\r\n3 \u00266\u0026 9\u0026 14 \\\\ \\hline\r\n12\u002615\u00264\u00267\\\\ \\hline \r\n5\u00262\u002613\u002610 \\\\ \\hline\r\n16\u002611\u00268\u00261 \\\\ \\hline\r\n\\end{array}\r\n\\]\r\nIf $n = 3$, then we need to check the statement for $k =3,5$: since there exists a closed knight's tour then there exists a $3$-prince's closed tour, while an example for $k=5$ is the following one:\r\n\\[\r\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|} \\hline\r\n9\u002612\u00265\u002624\u002629\u002618 \\\\ \\hline\r\n20\u002617\u002634\u002631\u002626\u002621 \\\\ \\hline\r\n15\u002622\u00261\u002610\u00267\u002614 \\\\ \\hline\r\n32\u002625\u002628\u002619\u00264\u002633 \\\\ \\hline\r\n3\u00268\u002613\u002616\u002635\u00262 \\\\ \\hline\r\n36\u002611\u00266\u002623\u002630\u002627 \\\\ \\hline\r\n\\end{array}\r\n\\]\r\nThe case $n=4$ follows from Proposition \\ref{prop:all8}.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-2","attach":3,"span":{"file":"paper.tex","from":90,"to":112},"hash":"sha256:b7334b355b8b40ea7e94a3736a3bb4f8c63d34bf38558d281ddccf113eb28bf3","minhash":"","census":"2307.16868v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2307.16868","version":"1","local":"thm-1","file":"","number":"1.3","section":"sec-1","order":6,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"Let $n$ be a positive integer, and let $k$ be any odd integer such that $n \\geq (k-1)(3k-2)$. Then, there exists a $k$P's tour over a $2n \\times 2n $ chessboard.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"thm","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"counter","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"paper.tex","from":115,"to":117},"hash":"sha256:b53d2b8f9c60f150690b4139b7d558c51eabf34cceaf21fca6ecf08f31ee774f","minhash":"","census":"2307.16868v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"problem","subkind":"problem","paper":"2307.16868","version":"1","local":"op-1","file":"","section":"sec-1","order":7,"title":"Open Problem","body_md":"Does there exist  a closed $k$P's tour over a $2n \\times 2n$ chessboard for every odd $k \\leq 2n-1$?","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"OP","item":"problem","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"paper.tex","from":119,"to":121},"hash":"sha256:e9525c32cbaa0981a9554ee93c7890b4333293ce8c26143795eec029b8b81769","minhash":"","census":"2307.16868v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
